WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:03.850
Speaker 1: Let's go next to Lahav from Greensboro.

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:05.439
Lahav, welcome.

00:00:05.439 --> 00:00:07.830
Speaker 5: Hello.

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:08.830
Can you hear me?

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:10.540
Speaker 1: Yes, I can.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:11.730
Speaker 5: Okay.

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:18.200
I'd like to ask about the system of government
that we have in the United States, because

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:23.130
in my opinion, you know, like multi-party
parliamentary systems, like in Germany or

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:24.130
Denmark.

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:25.130
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:26.529
Speaker 5: And other countries.

00:00:26.529 --> 00:00:33.051
Well, I think that's a preferable system because
mostly because of the multiparty aspect.

00:00:33.051 --> 00:00:36.010
I mean, I have issues with the two party duopoly.

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:41.160
I'm curious just what do you think of the
idea of a multi-party parliamentary system?

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:42.190
Could it work here?

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:47.199
Speaker 1: Well, whether it could work, I
mean, I think the answer is depends how hard

00:00:47.199 --> 00:00:48.199
we want to try.

00:00:48.199 --> 00:00:49.910
I think it of course, it could work.

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:53.010
I tend to like the multi-party parliamentary
system.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:59.479
You often hear as a stated downside of such
a system that it makes it too easy for extremists

00:00:59.479 --> 00:01:00.989
to get representation.

00:01:00.989 --> 00:01:09.260
So imagine that, you know, 5% of the vote
goes to the KKK party, and that means that

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:13.710
they would get 21 or some number of seats
in the House of Representatives or something

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:14.710
like that.

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:18.860
But I think there safeguards against that,
and I don't think it's as big a risk maybe

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:21.140
as as some people believe it to me.

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:27.290
I also see it as a way of, you know, we talk
all the time, how can we break the duopoly?

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:31.320
And often we say, well, you got to get rid
of the Electoral College, and then you got

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:35.790
to do ranked choice voting and fix campaign
finance, you know, all these different things.

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:39.700
A multi-party parliamentary system, at least
at the House of Representatives level, might

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.780
be a shortcut to breaking the two party duopoly.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:46.350
So I'm very positive for a number of reasons
on it.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:51.690
I don't I'm not as knowledgeable about it
as someone who maybe lives in such a system.

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:53.790
So there may be downsides I'm not considering.

00:01:53.790 --> 00:02:00.000
Speaker 5: Well, one downside and like Israel,
is the biggest downside I can think of, because

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.600
what they have is each country has a cut off
for how for what percentage of the vote you

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.310
have to get to a sea of seats in Israel.

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:08.720
It is way too low.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:12.609
You get like 20 parties in government and
an endless cycle of elections.

00:02:12.609 --> 00:02:13.609
Complete mess.

00:02:13.609 --> 00:02:14.609
Speaker 2: Right.

00:02:14.609 --> 00:02:16.620
Speaker 5: Other countries like Germany have
a higher cut off.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:17.879
Now, another issue.

00:02:17.879 --> 00:02:20.120
I actually support abolishing the Senate.

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:22.999
Like it's not Democratic in its current form.

00:02:22.999 --> 00:02:26.099
I don't even see a better form of it.

00:02:26.099 --> 00:02:29.430
And if there was a parliamentary system, I
don't see the point of a Senate.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:35.730
Speaker 1: Oh, I have ranted and raved about
the undemocratic nature of the Senate.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:42.450
I forget the exact numbers, but I think it's
something like the senators representing 17%

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:49.549
of the U.S. population can form a majority
because of how undemocratic it is.

00:02:49.549 --> 00:02:54.909
And I like you when I think about how you
fix the Senate.

00:02:54.909 --> 00:02:57.150
You're basically making it the House.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:58.150
So do you.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:02:59.900
Yeah, it's the Senate is a mess.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:00.900
I'm with you.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:06.909
Speaker 5: And final question, this one is
slightly unrelated, but I always get annoyed

00:03:06.909 --> 00:03:11.459
with the media when they pretend that both
sides are equal.

00:03:11.459 --> 00:03:15.640
And this is just another partizan fight and
it's really not.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:16.640
So how do you get that?

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:21.750
Sort of I'll call it the both sides culture
out of the media, particularly corporate media.

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:27.489
Speaker 1: You just have to repeat over and
over again, this is not a both sides thing.

00:03:27.489 --> 00:03:32.629
So when when corporate media has one person
that understands and accepts the science of

00:03:32.629 --> 00:03:37.819
climate change and another person who denies
it, and then you go, well, both sides, you

00:03:37.819 --> 00:03:44.309
guys kind of debate and see who convinces
who one side is not based in reality.

00:03:44.309 --> 00:03:49.220
And so that's just a matter of I think if
you if one is against in such a situation,

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:55.109
you have to repeat that there's a false neutrality
being created here that's deceptive, but you've

00:03:55.109 --> 00:03:57.019
just got to keep repeating it over and over
again.

00:03:57.019 --> 00:03:58.019
There's no other way.

00:03:58.019 --> 00:04:00.419
Speaker 5: More from their perspective.

00:04:00.419 --> 00:04:06.090
If someone, a climate deniers, is going to
go onto CNN and then the host will announce

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:09.099
that they're a liar, they're not going to
actually go on.

00:04:09.099 --> 00:04:14.359
So there might be a discreet way to stop these
people from actually showing up on cable news.

00:04:14.359 --> 00:04:17.120
Speaker 1: Yeah, I can't think of what it
is, but maybe you're right.

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:18.120
Maybe there is a way.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:19.240
Speaker 5: Okay.

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:20.900
Thank you for taking my call.

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:21.900
Speaker 1: My pleasure.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:27.640
There is Lahav from Greensboro, and that is
going to do it for calls today.

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:29.540
We will take calls again.

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:32.401
And I look forward to speaking to many more
of you next time.

